  "query_preview": "..\nIssued 145 PM CDT Thu Aug 27 2026\n\nSaturday through Thursday:\n\nSynopsis:\nThe longwave upper ridge axis currently over the Rocky \nMountains will build eastward into the midwest this weekend into\nearly next week, yielding return flow (warm and humid air) and \nactive weather. \n\nDiscussion:\nBroad low pressure over the Great Plains on Saturday will induce\na south breeze across the CWA. The nose of the 850mb LLJ builds\ninto western WI Saturday morning, yielding chances for showers\nunderneath it. Our CWA should remain predominantly dry through\nthe Saturday AM hours, with just a few shower chances near and\nnorth/west of the Dells). The LLJ and associated WAA gradually\ndrift east into the region later Saturday through early Sunday\nmorning, with showers likely to move in with it (50-65% rain\nchances, mainly overnight). The LLJ undergoes it's typical\nnocturnal intensification Saturday night, delivering very strong\n850-700mb WAA, but also leaving the mid level lapse rates and\ninstability fairly limited. Rumbles of thunder are certainly\npossible with this activity, but we have no concern for severe\nwx at this time. \n\nThe surface warmth and humidity (highs in the mid 80s and dewpoints \naround 70) arrive Sunday, with a continued southerly breeze. The \nnose of the LLJ (and the strongest WAA and highest precip chances) \ntrack northeastward out of our region, but the LLJ does remain \noverhead, with continued precip chances and sharpening mid level \nlapse rates / instability. From Sunday through midweek next week, \nECMWF and GEFS ensembles continue to resolve an active weather \npattern, with a combination of similar LLJ / WAA driven precip,\nas well as surface-850mb baroclinic zone driven precip, \nyielding occasional chances for showers and thunderstorms. PWAT \naround 1.75\" (briefly higher to 2\") and modest instability are \nresolved on some ensemble members. Locally heavy rainfall and \nstrong thunderstorms will be possible somewhere in the midwest \nregion, no clarity on exactly where or when just yet. The heat \nand humidity (highs in the mid to upper 80s, dewpoints around \n70) linger early next week, likely remaining above seasonal \nnorms through midweek. \n\nSheppard",
